<article class="env-lemma" id="0H0S">
  <p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/0H0S">Lemma <span data-tag="0H0S">21.43.5</span>.</a> In the situation above, suppose that $K$ is an object of $\mathit{QC}(\mathcal{O})$ and $M$ arbitrary in $D(\mathcal{O})$. For every object $U$ of $\mathcal{C}$ we have </p>
  <div class="equation">
  \[  \mathop{\mathrm{Hom}}\nolimits _{D(\mathcal{O}_ U)}(K|_ U, M|_ U) = R\mathop{\mathrm{Hom}}\nolimits _{\mathcal{O}(U)}(R\Gamma (U, K), R\Gamma (U, M))  \]
</div>
</article><article class="env-proof">
    <p>
      <strong>Proof.</strong>
      We may replace $\mathcal{C}$ by $\mathcal{C}/U$. Thus we may assume $U = X$ is a final object of $\mathcal{C}$. By Lemma <a href="/tag/0H0R" data-tag="0H0R">21.43.4</a> we see that $K = Lf^*P$ where $P = R\Gamma (U, K) = R\Gamma (X, K) = Rf_*K$. Thus the result because $Lf^*$ is the left adjoint to $Rf_*(-) = R\Gamma (U, -)$. 
      <span class="qed">$\square$</span>
    </p>
</article>